<configs>

	<!--
		=========================================================================
		THE MAIN MENU MOD SUMMARY PANEL
		=========================================================================

		The front-end counterpart to the in-game help screen: a tabbed text box on the
		main menu, one tab per contributed page. Same contract as the in-game screen - a
		contributor appends pages with one XPath, writes no C#, and never declares a tab
		button.

		[ WHAT IT IS FOR ]
		Summaries, not reference. This is the panel an overhaul uses to say what it is
		before anyone starts a game: what it changes, what to expect, anything worth
		deciding on up front. The in-game screen (ESC -> Mod Help) is where the detail
		lives, for someone already playing.

		[ HIDDEN UNTIL SOMETHING TURNS IT ON ]
		An empty box on the main menu helps nobody, and a lone About tab explaining that
		nothing has been contributed is worse than no box at all - so this window is
		defined here but registered nowhere. It belongs to no window_group, so nothing
		opens it and it does not exist as far as the menu is concerned. An overhaul opts
		in from its own XUi_Menu/xui.xml:

		    <append xpath="/xui/window_group[@name='mainMenu']">
		        <window name="mainMenuHelpScreens" />
		    </append>

		NOT a visible="false" attribute, which was tried and does not work:
		XUiV_Window.OnOpen sets base.IsVisible = true unconditionally whenever its group
		opens, so the XML value is discarded the moment the menu comes up. See the long
		note in xui.xml. Group membership is the switch.

		Enabling it is a statement of ownership. The panel holds four tabs and one is
		About, so whoever switches it on is taking responsibility for the other three -
		this is a front page for a pack, not a noticeboard every modlet pins to. A modlet
		can still contribute a tab without registering the window, and it simply stays
		unseen until some overhaul in the same install registers it.

		It is also deliberately flatter than the in-game screen. That one has two levels,
		a modlet column and a page strip, because it has 1260px to spend. This panel has
		450, so there is one level: every page is a top level tab, and contributors keep
		their captions short.

		[ PLACEMENT ]
		The menu's virtual canvas is 1920 wide and a window's origin is its top-left
		corner at pos, so anchor="Top" pos="420,-320" puts the left edge at x=420 - the
		same column vanilla's news boxes use (mainMenuNews sits at 870 with its "game"
		rect offset -450, giving 420..870). It ends at -880, level with the bottom of the
		button grid opposite (grid top -450 plus seven 60px rows).

		[ STRUCTURE ]
		headerbox, then a content rect carrying boxbg. That is the main menu's own panel
		shape, not an invention: vanilla's XUi_Menu uses headerbox 17 times and always in
		this pairing, with the content rect offset one pixel into the header

		    <headerbox height="40" text_key="..." />
		    <rect name="content" pos="0,-39" height="${parentinnerheight-40}" padding="10">
		        <boxbg ... />

		which is where the -39 and the -40 below come from. It is the same skeleton the
		0-SCore windows use in game, with headerbox standing in for the headerbg plus
		header.name label pair - those appear twice and once respectively in XUi_Menu, so
		using them here would read as an in-game dialog dropped onto the menu.

		headerbox brings the menu's red accent with it (border and text both 228,18,21),
		which is why the panel needs no separate title rule of its own.

		[ CHILD NAMES THAT MUST NOT CHANGE ]
		From the [XuiBindComponent] paths on XUiC_TabSelector - get them wrong and the
		selector logs an error and does nothing:
		  tabsHeader    searched recursively for TabSelectorButton controllers, and
		                non-recursively for the XUiV_Table that lays them out
		  tabsContents  searched recursively for TabSelectorTab controllers
		"border" and "backgroundMainTabs" are two more names it looks for - optional
		sprites it re-anchors, found by Single lookups that recurse first-match-wins. This
		panel defines neither. boxbg names its internals sprBorder and sprBackground, so
		the hazard is avoided by construction rather than by picking odd names.

		[ HOW BUTTONS APPEAR ]
		By INDEX. XUiC_TabSelector.Init pairs Tabs[i] with tabButtons[i], and each button
		takes its caption from its partner via {tab_name_localized}. Surplus buttons hide
		themselves (updateTabVisibility: i >= Tabs.Length -> IsVisible = false), so the
		eight declared below serve however many pages exist.

		Never let pages outnumber buttons: the surplus gets a null TabButton, which
		XUiC_TabSelectorTab.TabSelected dereferences unguarded.

		[ CAPACITY ]
		Four tabs, from cols="4" on the button grid. Unlike the in-game nav column this
		strip does not scroll, and unlike the table it replaced it does not pack buttons
		at their natural width - every cell is a fixed 107px. A caption too long for one
		shrinks to fit rather than overrunning its neighbour, so a long mod name costs
		legibility rather than layout.

		Prefer the modlet's own name over a topic: one summary tab per modlet is the
		intent, and the About tab holds one of the four.

		Raising it is a one-liner from a contributor's own file, with cell_width brought
		down to match:

		    <set xpath="/windows/window[@name='mainMenuHelpScreens']//grid[@name='tabButtons']/@cols">5</set>
		    <set xpath="/windows/window[@name='mainMenuHelpScreens']//grid[@name='tabButtons']/@cell_width">86</set>

		Past five or so the cells stop being wide enough to read. There is no button to
		append alongside it - see the note on the grid itself.

		[ WHY A DEFAULT PAGE SHIPS, AND WHAT TO DO WITH IT ]
		XUiC_TabSelector logs an error and bails out entirely on zero tabs, so the panel
		needs at least one child of its own. The About page below is that floor, and
		doubles as the worked example a contributor copies.

		Tab 0 is selected at Init, so About is what opens - which makes it the right home
		for the general description of the pack rather than a note about the panel. The
		body shipped here is a placeholder saying as much, and whoever enables the panel
		is expected to replace it:

		    <set xpath="/windows/window[@name='mainMenuHelpScreens']//helpmenu_page[@tab_key='helpMenuAbout']/@text_key">yourPackOverviewBody</set>

		That patch reaches a template INSTANTIATION, not a template's internals - the
		instantiation is an ordinary node carrying ordinary attributes right up until
		templates expand, and XPath patching runs before that. The same timing is why
		nothing can append into a template's insides.

		Leaving About generic and spending a separate tab on the overview also works, but
		costs one of the four cells and puts the pack's own words one click away from
		where the panel opens.

		select_tab_contents_on_open / select_tab_contents_on_change are off because the
		tab bodies hold no selectable controls for gamepad focus to land on. Note the
		second name: vanilla's own sandboxOptions and serverBrowser windows write it as
		"select_contents_on_change", which is not the registered attribute name
		(XUiC_TabSelector.attributeSelectTabContentsOnChange) and so does nothing. The
		spelling below is the one the code actually reads.

		Gamepad: with use_page_buttons left at its default false, XUiC_TabSelector.Update
		cycles tabs on GUI Window Paging Left/Right while the main menu group is open.
		Nothing else in the main menu binds those, so there is no conflict. Setting it
		true moves the binding to Page Up/Down and makes the window request gamepad
		callout icons, which would put extra chrome on the main menu - hence off.

		[ HISTORY ]
		This panel was mainMenuLearnByDoing, owned by SphereII Learn By Doing, with its
		three tabs written as literal XML and its own lbd_tab_button template. It now
		lives here so that any modlet can add a page, and Learn By Doing contributes to
		it the same way every other modlet does.
	-->
	<append xpath="/windows">

		<window name="mainMenuHelpScreens" anchor="Top" pos="420,-320" width="450" height="560" depth="1">

			<!--
				Title bar. helpMenuTitle is its own key rather than the helpScreensTitle the
				in-game screen uses, because that one also captions the ESC menu button -
				the two surfaces are named separately on purpose.

				height 40 and font_size 26 are the scaled-down settings: headerbox defaults
				to 50 and font_size 38, which are sized for the full width menu pages, not a
				450px side panel. Vanilla uses height="40" the same way for its nested
				panels.
			-->
			<headerbox height="40" font_size="26" text_key="helpMenuTitle" />

			<!--
				CHROME. 16,16,16 with a 2px [darkGrey] border is the menu's content panel
				styling - 21 and 23 uses respectively in XUi_Menu, the most common of each,
				and always on exactly this <rect name="content"> block.

				It is deliberately NOT pure black. [black] is 0,0,0,255 and belongs to the
				news boxes, which are image backed and want a hard backing behind a photo;
				borrowing from them made this panel read as a darker hole than everything
				around it. 16,16,16 is the near-black the menu uses for panels you read text
				in. The border is [darkGrey] (64,64,64) rather than [mediumGrey] (96,96,96)
				for the same reason - the two go together, and moving one without the other
				leaves a pairing vanilla never uses.

				boxbg self sizes from outerwidth/outerheight with ignoreparentpadding, so
				the frame follows this rect rather than being restated beside it.
			-->
			<rect name="content" pos="0,-39" height="${parentinnerheight-40}" depth="1" padding="10">

				<boxbg backcolor="16,16,16,255" bordersprite="menu_empty2px" bordercolor="[darkGrey]" />

				<rect name="tabs" depth="4" width="${parentinnerwidth}" height="${parentinnerheight}"
				      controller="TabSelector"
				      select_tab_contents_on_open="false" select_tab_contents_on_change="false">

					<!--
						Tab strip. One declaration, exactly as both in-game grids do it: the
						grid carries repeat_content and stamps out rows*cols copies, so
						neither this file nor a contributor ever lists a button.

						This is why it is a grid and not the XUiV_Table it used to be.
						repeat_content is declared on XUiView, so any view accepts the
						attribute - but the count comes from XUiView.RepeatCount, which is
						virtual and resets to 1, and only XUiV_Grid overrides it (Columns *
						Rows). A table carrying repeat_content therefore yields a single
						button, which is why the strip previously had eight listed by hand.

						The trade is fixed cells instead of text-width packing: 4 x 107
						across the 430 content width, 2px spare. A caption longer than the
						cell shrinks to fit rather than overrunning its neighbour - see the
						template. Keep captions short anyway; a mod's own name is the
						intent, and a long one will shrink noticeably.

						Capacity is now the cols count and nothing else, so raising it is a
						genuine one-liner from a contributor's own file, with no matching
						button to append:

						    <set xpath="/windows/window[@name='mainMenuHelpScreens']//grid[@name='tabButtons']/@cols">5</set>

						though cell_width has to come down to match, and past five or so the
						cells stop being wide enough to read.
					-->
					<rect name="tabsHeader" pos="0,0" width="${parentinnerwidth}" height="34" depth="5">
						<grid name="tabButtons" pos="0,0" depth="5" rows="1" cols="4"
						      cell_width="107" cell_height="32" repeat_content="true"
						      arrangement="horizontal">
							<helpmenu_tabbutton />
						</grid>
					</rect>

					<sprite depth="5" name="headerRule" pos="0,-38" width="${parentinnerwidth}" height="2"
					        sprite="menu_empty2px" type="sliced" color="[red]" globalopacitymod="0" />

					<!--
						One page per contributor. A contributor appends a sibling of the page
						below with:

						  <append xpath="/windows/window[@name='mainMenuHelpScreens']//rect[@name='tabsContents']">
						      <helpmenu_page tab_key="..." text_key="..." />
						  </append>

						wrapped in <conditional><if cond="mod_loaded('sphereii_help_screens')">,
						because this window only exists when this modlet is installed.

						SIZING: this rect starts below the rule at -46 and runs to the bottom
						of the padded content area, so 430 across and 500-46 down. helpmenu_page
						declares those two numbers literally, the way helpscreen_page does in
						XUi_InGame, so that a contributor never reasons about sizing - which
						means the template's defaults and this rect have to be changed together.
					-->
					<rect name="tabsContents" pos="0,-46" width="${parentinnerwidth}"
					      height="${parentinnerheight-46}" depth="5">

						<helpmenu_page tab_key="helpMenuAbout" text_key="helpMenuAboutBody" />

					</rect>
				</rect>
			</rect>
		</window>

	</append>

</configs>
